<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  What a witness claims about a corpus of questions.

  Copyright (c) 2026 Kenny Flegal. Licensed under MIT OR Apache-2.0.

  ⚠️ PROVISIONAL NAMESPACE URI, same as the base schema.

  ⛔⛔ SCOPE, AND IT IS NARROWER THAN IT LOOKS. Three objects exist in an assertion
  system: the QUESTION, the CLAIM about it, and the RUN that happened. This schema
  carries the first two and DELIBERATELY NOT THE THIRD.

  ⭐⭐ AND A FOURTH ARRIVED WITH `dependence`, WHICH IS THE CLAIM ROW POINTED OUTWARD: a
  claim about TWO OTHER PARTIES' FILINGS, made by somebody who filed neither. It is here
  and not in the base schema for the reason that decides everything in this file: a
  filing is attestable by the party that files it, so an observation spanning two filings
  belongs to the third party who read both. See Dependence.

    the question   stays in each corpus. Datetime formats, facility cases and plan
                   claims are different subjects; a shared corpus would be a shared
                   subject. Only its PROVENANCE is unified here, as Citation.
    the claim      ⭐ IS THIS SCHEMA'S REASON TO EXIST. It crosses organisations: an
                   accountant's answers to a corpus ARE a coverage file, and they
                   should not have to run anybody's code to send one.
    the run        ⭐ HERE, BUT ONLY IN ITS PROMOTED FORM, AND THE DISTINCTION IS
                   LOAD-BEARING. A runner's working ledger is a local, append-only
                   file answering "when did this go red?", machine-local, growing
                   forever, and NEVER a shareable artifact or a gate predicate. This
                   schema does not describe it. What it describes is a run PROMOTED
                   TO EVIDENCE: the extract a report cites, dated, frozen and sent.
                   ⛔ Emitting the working ledger in this format would be a category
                   error: it would look citable and would not be.

  ⛔ AND NO RUNNER IS DESCRIBED. Unification here is BY CONFORMANCE, NOT BY
  DEPENDENCY: a shared vocabulary plus a test per runner that it conforms, never a
  library everything imports. Two models that share a code path cannot corroborate
  each other, so the whole point is that a witness imports nothing from here.
-->
<xs:schema xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
           xmlns:asrt="https://example.invalid/assertion/1.0"
           xmlns:pm="https://example.invalid/process-flow/1.0"
           targetNamespace="https://example.invalid/assertion/1.0"
           elementFormDefault="qualified"
           version="0.1.0">

  <xs:import namespace="https://example.invalid/process-flow/1.0"
             schemaLocation="process-modulus.xsd"/>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       ANSWER: what one witness says about one question.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:complexType name="Answer">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔⛔⛔ BOTH ANSWERS THAT CARRY A VALUE CARRY A **BORROWED** ONE, AND THAT IS THE
      WHOLE CORRECTION THIS SCHEMA MAKES. A refusal code and a chart position are
      both drawn from a list SOMEBODY ELSE PUBLISHES, and a witness under a different
      regime draws from a different list.

        holds      Recognised, and WHERE: a position in a chart of accounts. The
                   chart is the taxonomy. A US chart and a Portuguese one do not
                   share a single code, so `1200` alone names nothing.
        refuses    ⭐ THE DELIVERABLE. A TYPED refusal, drawn from the coding pack
                   the accountants hold. That pack is the authority, not this
                   schema, and not any witness's implementation of it.
        cannotAsk  ⛔ NEITHER A FAILURE NOR A REFUSAL. The witness has no opinion on
                   this kind of question. A standards model asked about staffing
                   policy answers this and is right to.

      ⚠️ WHAT THIS REPLACES, AND WHY IT WAS A FORK WAITING TO HAPPEN. The
      implementation this came from held the six refusal codes as a compiled array of
      bare strings, with a comment saying the authority was the accountants' pack.
      **A comment naming an authority beside a hard-coded copy of its list is a fork
      that has not drifted yet.** Add a second regime and there are two bad options:
      widen the array and lose which word belongs to whom, or fork it.

      ⭐⭐ AND THE SHARED-VOCABULARY PROPERTY SURVIVES THE CHANGE, WHICH IS THE POINT.
      Two answers are comparable row for row when they cite the SAME taxonomy, and
      now two answers under DIFFERENT taxonomies are legible as different rather than
      silently incomparable. That is strictly more than the array could say.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="holds"     type="pm:BorrowedTerm"/>
      <xs:element name="refuses"   type="pm:BorrowedTerm"/>
      <xs:element name="cannotAsk" type="asrt:Nothing"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Nothing">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      An answer with no payload. It exists because XSD 1.0 has no empty-branch choice,
      and because `cannotAsk` genuinely carries nothing: inventing a field for it
      would invite somebody to fill one in.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       VERDICT: how an answer stood against the corpus. CONTRIBUTED, and
       regime-neutral: it is about the corpus and the witness, not about
       any accounting framework.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:simpleType name="Verdict">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      agreed     Claimed, and the answer matches the corpus (or its declared
                 exception).

      diverged   Claimed, and the answer differs with no exception. ⛔ THERE ARE
                 EXACTLY THREE LEGITIMATE RESPONSES: fix the witness, fix the case,
                 or declare the exception. Editing the expected answer to match what
                 the witness happens to do is none of them: it turns the corpus into
                 a snapshot of current behaviour and the trial measures nothing.

      notable    ⭐⭐ NOT CLAIMED, AND THE WITNESS ANSWERED ANYWAY. Never a failure,
                 and usually the most valuable line in a report: a witness admitted
                 something we assumed it must refuse, so either the model is stronger
                 than we thought or a guard is not guarding.

      unclaimed  Not claimed, and the witness said cannotAsk. Quiet, and correct.

      ⚠️ Verdict is NOT the absence vocabulary. `pm:AbsenceReason` says why a DOCUMENT
      has no value; this says how an ANSWERER stood against a question. They rhyme
      and they are different axes; merging them is the error this project keeps
      catching one level down.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:restriction base="xs:token">
      <xs:enumeration value="agreed"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="diverged"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="notable"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="unclaimed"/>
    </xs:restriction>
  </xs:simpleType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       CITATION: a question's or an answer's authority, named.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:complexType name="Citation">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      WHICH CLAUSE, IN WHICH VERSION, DECIDED THIS.

      `instrument` is the standard or pack, as a BorrowedTerm so the naming authority
      is explicit. `clause` is the part of it: "606-10-25-1", "§4.2". `version` is
      the edition READ, because a standard is not a fixed object and a citation
      without a year is a citation to whatever is current.

      ⛔ THIS IS THE FIELD MOST LIKELY TO BE LEFT EMPTY AND MOST EXPENSIVE WHEN IT IS.
      A corpus whose questions cite nothing cannot be checked against the instrument
      it claims to test; it can only be checked against itself. Optional here because
      a required element that senders cannot fill produces invented values, but a
      claim with no citation is weaker, and a receiver is entitled to say so.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="instrument" type="pm:BorrowedTerm"/>
      <xs:element name="clause"     type="xs:token"  minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="version"    type="xs:token"  minOccurs="0"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       COVERAGE: the claim. One witness, one corpus, question by question.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:complexType name="CoverageEntry">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      `key` names a question in the corpus this coverage answers. ⚠️ Keys are the
      corpus's, and this schema cannot validate them; a runner must, and refusing a
      claim about a question the corpus does not hold is what stops a coverage file
      drifting into fiction.

      `complete` is whether the witness claims to answer the question FULLY. ⭐ It is
      what makes `notable` reachable: without a claim there is nothing for an answer
      to exceed, and a trial that cannot produce `notable` is only checking that a
      program agrees with itself.

      ⭐⭐ `answer` IS OPTIONAL, AND WHICH WAY IT IS FILLED SAYS WHAT KIND OF WITNESS
      THIS IS:

        absent   the witness is a PROGRAM. Run it; this file only says what it
                 claims, and the answer comes from the run.
        present  ⭐ THE WITNESS IS THE FILE. An accountant answering the corpus on
                 paper, or a regime nobody has written code for yet, states the
                 answer here and needs no runner at all. This is how a jurisdiction
                 arrives before an implementation of it does.

      `exception` is the reason this witness's answer differs from the corpus ON
      PURPOSE, and it is only meaningful beside an `answer`. ⛔ XSD 1.0 cannot express
      "only with", so a runner must refuse the combination. AND AN UNEXPLAINED
      DIVERGENCE IS NOT CONFORMANT: a difference with no reason is indistinguishable
      from a bug somebody stopped chasing.

      ⚠️ A regime that genuinely disagrees with the corpus is THE FINDING. A regime
      that quietly rewrites the corpus to agree with itself is a fork.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="key"       type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="complete"  type="xs:boolean"/>
      <xs:element name="answer"    type="asrt:Answer"   minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="exception" type="xs:string"   minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="citation"  type="asrt:Citation" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Coverage">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⭐⭐⭐ A WITNESS IS NOT REQUIRED TO BE A PROGRAM. When an accountant answers the
      corpus on paper, their answers ARE a coverage file, and that is the reason this
      is a document format rather than a Rust trait. A second regime should be able to
      answer without running, compiling or depending on anything of ours.

      `regime` is `pm:Regime`, reused rather than re-stated: a witness reports under a
      framework exactly the way a document does, and the same three-axis trap applies
      (jurisdiction · framework · the authority coding it). ⭐ TWO REGIMES ARE
      LEGITIMATE HERE FOR THE SAME REASON THEY ARE THERE: one witness may answer
      under a framework coded by two authorities that do not line up.

      ⚠️ `regime` is optional, and its absence is a real state rather than an
      oversight: a witness that is not an accounting model (a register census, a
      spreadsheet-formula checker) reports under no framework and should not be made
      to invent one.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="witness" type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="regime"  type="pm:Regime"      minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
      <xs:element name="entry"   type="asrt:CoverageEntry" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       RUN: a trial, promoted to evidence.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:complexType name="Result">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      One question, one witness, one verdict, and where it matters, what the
      witness actually said.

      ⭐ `answer` is here as well as on a coverage entry because they are different
      facts: there, it is what the witness CLAIMS; here, it is what the run OBSERVED.
      A promoted run whose answers were copied from the claim would be evidence of
      nothing, and keeping both is what makes `diverged` checkable after the fact.

      `evidence` is the human-readable trace, the line a reader follows to satisfy
      themselves the verdict is real. ⛔ A `diverged` with no evidence is an assertion
      about the past that nobody can check, which is the state a promoted record
      exists to prevent.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="key"      type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="verdict"  type="asrt:Verdict"/>
      <xs:element name="answer"   type="asrt:Answer" minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="evidence" type="xs:string"   minOccurs="0"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Run">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔⛔ THIS IS THE PROMOTED RECORD, NOT THE LEDGER. A runner accumulates results
      locally, forever, on one machine; that file is a working record and a claim
      made from it is a claim about one machine. THIS is what you get when a report
      argues from a run: an extract, dated, that travels with the argument.

      ⭐ `ranAt` is REQUIRED, and it is the difference between the two. A working
      ledger's dates are per-line and internal; a promoted record is a statement
      about a moment, and a promotion with no date cannot be compared to the next
      one, which is the entire reason to promote it.

      ⚠️ `corpusVersion` names WHICH corpus was answered. A verdict is meaningless
      against an unnamed corpus: the questions change, and "it went green" without
      saying what it was asked is not a result.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="witness"       type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="ranAt"         type="xs:date"/>
      <xs:element name="corpusVersion" type="xs:token"    minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="result"        type="asrt:Result" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:element name="run" type="asrt:Run">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⭐ A run promoted to evidence. ⛔ NOT a runner's working ledger. See the type.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:key name="resultKey">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:result"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:key"/>
    </xs:key>
  </xs:element>

  <xs:element name="coverage" type="asrt:Coverage">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      One witness's claim about one corpus. ⛔ A coverage file with no entries is an
      error rather than a witness that claims nothing: a corpus of nothing runs no
      trials and reports success, which is how a green suite comes to mean nothing.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:key name="entryKey">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:entry"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:key"/>
    </xs:key>
    <xs:key name="regimeId">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:regime"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="pm:id"/>
    </xs:key>
  </xs:element>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       DEPENDENCE: an observed dependence between TWO FILINGS, filed by
       somebody who is the filer of neither.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:complexType name="FiledLayer">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ONE END: WHOSE FILING, WHICH FILING, WHICH EDITION OF IT, AND WHERE IN IT.

      ⭐⭐ FOUR FACTS, AND THAT IS WHY THIS IS A TYPE RATHER THAN A STRING. "Acme Sub's
      labour layer in the 2026-Q2 stack" is a party, a document, an edition and a
      position inside it. One token carrying all four is joinable on none of them, and a
      receiver holding fifty of these cannot ask "everything touching Acme Sub" without
      matching text.

      `filing` is `pm:ForeignId`, used exactly as its own annotation describes it: "a
      notation plus an id names a POSITION IN ONE FILE that no authority publishes". The
      notation is the URI of the OTHER FILING; the id is the layer name inside it. ⭐ It
      is that type's second use and its first as a reference TARGET rather than a field,
      and it needed no widening: a layer name is precisely a position no authority
      publishes, which is why `pm:ForeignId` and not `pm:BorrowedTerm`.

      ⛔⛔ NOTHING HERE VALIDATES THAT THE FILING EXISTS, THAT THE URI RESOLVES, OR THAT
      THE LAYER IS IN IT. ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. An XSD identity constraint is
      scoped to one document, and a keyref that appeared to reach across one would be the
      exact defect this document type exists to avoid: a reference that looks constrained
      and is not. An implementer that can fetch the other filing still owes the check; one
      that cannot owes the reader the knowledge that it did not happen.

      `party` is REQUIRED. ⭐ Without it two ends could be two filings of ONE entity and a
      reader could not tell, which silently loses the fact this whole document type exists
      for: THE TWO ENDS CANNOT SEE EACH OTHER.

      `registration` is that party in a register that publishes one (an LEI, a national
      tax number, an EUID) as a `pm:BorrowedTerm`, so the register is NAMED and two
      spellings of one name become joinable rather than undetectable. It is S-3's argument
      about signers, applied to entities. ⚠️ Optional, and deliberately unwrapped: the term
      is an ADDITION to the identity rather than the FRAME it is read under, so its silence
      says only "not cited". ⛔ Required would be wrong at exactly the end of the chain this
      model cares about (the small supplier with no register entry), and would produce
      invented identifiers.

      `version` is THE EDITION OF THE FILING THAT WAS READ, and it is `Citation/version`'s
      argument applied to a filing rather than a standard: a stack is not a fixed object,
      and a reference with no edition is a reference to whatever is current. ⚠️ It sits
      BESIDE `filing` rather than inside it because `pm:ForeignId` is shared with the base
      schema, and a document type does not get to widen a shared type for its own
      convenience.

      `regime` is a DOCUMENT-LOCAL HANDLE naming which of THIS statement's declared regimes
      that filing reported under. ⭐ It is the one reference in this type a validator can
      check, and it IS checked. See the keyrefs on the element. ⚠️ It is not a copy of the
      other filing's declaration presented as that entity's own fact: the observer RESTATES
      it, dated by `observedAt`, which is `Holder`'s answer to a cached value nothing
      refreshes.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="party"        type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="registration" type="pm:BorrowedTerm" minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="filing"       type="pm:ForeignId"/>
      <xs:element name="version"      type="xs:token" minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="regime"       type="xs:token" minOccurs="0"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="DependenceEntry">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ONE PAIR, AND WHAT WAS SEEN.

      `observed` is REQUIRED, and the sentence is `pm:Coupling`'s carried over word for
      word on purpose: A COUPLING FILED WITHOUT SAYING WHAT WAS OBSERVED IS AN OPINION, and
      the one element whose purpose is to carry a refutation is the last place to accept
      one. ⛔⛔ IT IS LESS NEGOTIABLE HERE, NOT MORE. There, a reader who doubts the
      observation can read the rest of the filing it sits in. Here BOTH ENDS ARE ELSEWHERE,
      so this element is the entire evidence a receiver holds.

      ⛔ AND IT IS NOT ABSENT-ABLE. A typed absence on `observed` would be an opinion
      wearing a type, strictly worse than an untyped one, because it would look conformant.

      `strength` is `pm:StatedClaim` and OPTIONAL, identical to `pm:Coupling/strength`, for
      the reason `Remainder/sign` gives: THE DIRECTION IS FREQUENTLY KNOWN WHEN THE MAGNITUDE
      IS NOT. "The subsidiary ran hot whenever the parent cut its block" is a filable
      observation with no number in it. ⚠️ Requiring it here was considered and DECLINED: the
      two elements carry the SAME FACT, and holding the cross-filing one to a stricter
      standard would say the asymmetry between them is about MEASUREMENT when it is about
      ATTESTATION. If `pm:Coupling/strength` is ever promoted to required, this moves with it.

      ⭐ `from` AND `to` ARE DIRECTED, AND THE DIRECTION IS A CLAIM. "Relieving the supplier's
      quantum reduced the customer's remainder" is a different statement from its reverse, and
      an observer who files the wrong one has filed a wrong thing rather than a vague one.
      ⚠️ An observer who has comovement and NOT direction has a weaker observation and should
      say so in `observed` rather than picking an order quietly. If that turns out to be the
      common case, two unordered ends plus a typed direction is the change to argue, on
      evidence, and not here.

      ⛔ `from` AND `to` MUST NOT BE THE SAME FILING AND THE SAME LAYER. A layer is not
      coupled to itself. ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. XSD 1.0 has no `xs:assert` and
      cannot compare across elements. An implementer still owes this check; the schema cannot
      take it from them.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="from"     type="asrt:FiledLayer"/>
      <xs:element name="to"       type="asrt:FiledLayer"/>
      <xs:element name="observed" type="xs:string"/>
      <xs:element name="strength" type="pm:StatedClaim" minOccurs="0"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Dependence">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔⛔⛔ AN OBSERVED DEPENDENCE BETWEEN TWO LAYERS THAT ARE FILED SEPARATELY, AND WHAT
      MAKES IT A DOCUMENT RATHER THAN AN ELEMENT IS ATTESTATION, NOT SHAPE.

      `pm:Coupling` is the same phenomenon inside one filing, and it exists so the model can
      be refuted in its own format. But the dependence that matters most is frequently
      between TWO ENTITIES THAT FILE SEPARATELY AND CANNOT SEE EACH OTHER: a parent and a
      subsidiary, a supplier and a customer, two borrowers of one lender.

      ⛔⛔ AND THE OBVIOUS REPAIR IS THE WRONG ONE, RECORDED HERE SO IT IS NOT RE-PROPOSED.
      Widening `pm:Coupling/from` and `/to` to accept a `pm:ForeignId` was considered and
      REJECTED. A FILING IS ATTESTABLE BY THE PARTY THAT FILES IT, and a coupling inside
      entity A's document naming entity B's layer is a claim A CANNOT ATTEST TO, because A
      cannot see B's stack. ⛔ WORSE: `couplingFrom` and `couplingTo` could not follow it, SO
      IT WOULD VALIDATE BY NOT BEING CHECKED: a reference that looks constrained and is not,
      while the two keyrefs went on reporting success about the half they could still reach.
      ⚠️ The two workarounds available before this element are worse again: fabricating one
      `Stack` spanning both entities asserts a shared system that does not exist and that
      neither filer could attest to, and a stub layer files a layer nobody has.

      ⭐⭐ SO THE THIRD PARTY FILES IT, AND THIS SCHEMA ALREADY HAS THAT GENRE. `Coverage` and
      `Run` are claims ABOUT things rather than elements INSIDE them, and each carries a
      `witness`. A group consolidator, a statutory auditor, a lender reading two borrowers:
      each has read both filings, NEITHER FILER HAS, and the observation is theirs to stand
      behind. ⭐ The world already files it this way: a consolidation is a separate
      statement, not a footnote in the subsidiary's accounts.

      ⭐⭐ BOTH ENDS ARE FOREIGN, ALWAYS, AND THAT IS NOT A LIMITATION. IT IS WHAT MAKES THE
      DESIGN WORK. This document is inside neither filing, so an auditor's own client's stack
      is as external to THIS statement as the counterparty's. There is never one local end
      beside one foreign one, and therefore never a keyref that must reach across a boundary
      and cannot. Every reference here is either wholly local and checked (the regime handles)
      or wholly foreign and honestly unchecked (the filings).

      ⚠️ A WITNESS WHO IS THE FILER OF BOTH ENDS IS IN THE WRONG DOCUMENT. Their own
      observation belongs in `pm:Coupling`, inside the filing they attest to. ⛔ NOT REACHABLE
      BY A VALIDATOR: nothing here knows who filed what. ⭐ But `from` and `to` MAY name the
      same filing: an auditor who sees a coupling between two layers of one client's stack has
      an observation the client did not file and cannot be made to file. The distinguishing
      fact is WHO OBSERVED, not where the ends are.

      `witness` is REQUIRED, exactly as on `Coverage` and `Run`, and for a sharper reason than
      either: THIS IS TESTIMONY ABOUT PARTIES WHO DID NOT CONSENT TO IT, and anonymous
      testimony about somebody else's books is not a filing.

      ⭐ `observedAt` is REQUIRED, and it is `Run/ranAt`'s argument rather than `Coverage`'s. A
      coverage claim is a standing position; THIS IS A STATEMENT ABOUT A MOMENT. Both filings
      change, so an observation that does not say when it was made cannot be checked against
      the filings as they were, nor compared with the next one, which is the entire reason to
      file it. ⚠️ One date and not an interval, for `Jagged`'s stated reason: time is an axis
      on a simulation of this model, not a field on its types.

      `provenance` is where a firm's own chain goes: who entered, who approved, and above all
      `standing`, which is the axis separating a statutory auditor's observation from a
      controller's hunch, and which is a BorrowedTerm so neither this schema nor any witness
      owns that set. ⚠️ Optional, because `witness` already forbids silence and every field
      inside `Provenance` is itself optional, so requiring the container would buy an empty
      tag. ⚠️ `witness` AND `provenance/party` ARE NOT ONE FACT WITH TWO HOMES: the first is
      the observer as named across this file's documents, the second is the entity standing
      behind it, and they differ whenever the observer is a team or a tool inside a firm, the
      same split `Holder` draws between kind and party.

      `regime` is `pm:Regime`, unbounded and optional, exactly as on `Coverage`, and it is the
      frameworks THIS OBSERVER computed remainders under. ⭐⭐ IT IS ALSO WHAT MAKES TWO ENDS
      UNDER DIFFERENT FRAMEWORKS LEGIBLE, WHICH IS THE CONSOLIDATOR'S ORDINARY CASE: a
      Portuguese subsidiary under NCRF-PE and a US parent under US-GAAP measure a remainder
      differently, and a document that cannot say so has hidden the reason the two numbers
      disagree. Each end names WHICH declared regime its filing reported under. ⛔ THE
      ALTERNATIVE, declaring both and explaining which is which inside `observed`, IS THE
      ERROR `Absence` ALREADY NAMES: a reason no query can reach is not a typed absence, and a
      regime attribution no query can reach is not an attribution.

      `citation` names the instrument under which this observer could see BOTH filings: a
      group-audit standard, a consolidation standard, an engagement. ⚠️ It is the nearest
      thing to an answer to "why should a reader believe you saw both?", which is the question
      this document type raises and no other element answers. Optional for `Citation`'s own
      stated reason: a required element senders cannot fill produces invented values. A
      dependence with no citation is weaker, and a receiver is entitled to say so.

      ⚠️ ONE STATEMENT, ONE MOMENT, MANY OBSERVATIONS. A consolidator reading a group files
      one document with an entry per pair, the way a run files a result per question.
      Observations made on different days are different statements, for the same reason a
      promotion is dated.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="witness"    type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="observedAt" type="xs:date"/>
      <xs:element name="provenance" type="pm:Provenance"        minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="regime"     type="pm:Regime"            minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
      <xs:element name="citation"   type="asrt:Citation"        minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
      <xs:element name="entry"      type="asrt:DependenceEntry"               maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:element name="dependence" type="asrt:Dependence">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⭐ ONE OBSERVER'S STATEMENT THAT TWO SEPARATELY FILED STACKS MOVE TOGETHER.

      ⛔ NOT `pm:Coupling`, AND NOT `pm:Coupling` WIDENED. See the type. A coupling belongs
      inside the filing whose filer can attest to both ends; this exists for the case where
      NOBODY WHO CAN FILE IT CAN ATTEST TO IT.

      ⛔ A dependence document with no entries is an error rather than an observer who
      observed nothing. An empty statement about two filings reports success at having
      looked, which is how a document that means nothing comes to be cited.

      ⭐ THE ONLY REFERENCES CHECKED HERE ARE THE ONES THAT CAN BE. The regime handles are
      document-local and a stock XSD 1.0 validator resolves them. ⛔ THE FILINGS ARE NOT AND
      CANNOT BE: that is the whole reason this is a separate document, and pretending
      otherwise with a keyref would reintroduce the defect it was created to avoid.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>

    <!-- ⚠️ NOT `regimeId`: identity-constraint names share one symbol space per target
         namespace, and `asrt:coverage` already declares one. -->
    <xs:key name="dependenceRegimeId">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:regime"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="pm:id"/>
    </xs:key>

    <xs:keyref name="fromRegime" refer="asrt:dependenceRegimeId">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:entry/asrt:from"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:regime"/>
    </xs:keyref>

    <xs:keyref name="toRegime" refer="asrt:dependenceRegimeId">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:entry/asrt:to"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:regime"/>
    </xs:keyref>

    <!-- One directed pair, one entry. Two observations about one pair by one witness on one
         date belong in one entry whose `observed` says both things; otherwise `strength`
         has no domain. A→B and B→A are different sequences and both stay legal: a mutual
         dependence is a legitimate filing. -->
    <xs:key name="dependencePair">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:entry"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:from/asrt:filing/pm:notation"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:from/asrt:filing/pm:id"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:to/asrt:filing/pm:notation"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:to/asrt:filing/pm:id"/>
    </xs:key>
  </xs:element>

</xs:schema>
